{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n40 \n \n \nThe abstract from this submission states: \nThis study is an examination of steady-state behavior in spherical diffusion flames \nin microgravity on the International Space Station (ISS). Gaseous spherical \ndiffusion flames stabilized on a porous sphere were employed in a normal \nconfiguration (i.e., fuel issuing into an ambient oxidizer). The use of a porous \nsphere allowed for a spherical flame with a constant mass flow rate, a condition \nthat is not attainable with free-droplet combustion.  Unlike previous microgravity \nspherical flame studies, time is not a limitation for these experiments, and the \npotential to achieve steady-state flames can be explored for the first time.   \nStoichiometric mixture fraction, Zst, and flame temperature were varied by \ndiluting the fuel with nitrogen, or changing the ambient oxygen concentration, \neither 21 or 40 vol% oxygen. Flame size was found to increase with increasing fuel \nflow rate and decrease with increasing ambient oxygen concentration. The large \nspherical diffusion flames were observed to grow until extinction, while small \nflames were found to exhibit an asymptotically steady behavior due to their low \nradiative losses and faster transport.  Experimental measurements of flame size \nwere in quantitative agreement with numerical results with one-step chemistry, \nprovided the diffusivity was appropriately adjusted. For the small flame sizes that \nare necessary to achieve steady-state flames, the burner acts as a heat sink, and \nas the burner temperature increases, its cooling effect on the flame diminishes, \nand the flame temperature increases. Accordingly, fiber temperatures measured \nusing two-color ratio pyrometry showed increasing temperature with time for the \nflames examined. Flame emissions of OH* (310 nm) measured with a \nphotomultiplier tube and a bandpass filter showed a steady intensity after the \ninitial ignition transient. The flames investigated appear to be asymptotically \napproaching a steady-state size and temperature, however, a decisive conclusion \ncannot be made because of burner heating. \n \nEarly Flame Design results from the ISS testing were presented in the following oral \npresentations: \n1. Richard Axelbaum, Peter Sunderland, and David Urban, \u201cACME - Flame Design,\u201d \nISS Increments 61 & 62 Science Symposium, 17-19 Sept. 2019. \n2. P. Irace, R.L. Axelbaum, and P.B. Sunderland, \u201cRadiative and Kinetic Extinction of", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 40}}